h function—I could implement all of them together within the line budget, with each utility taking only 30-50 lines of production code. the PRD files by their likely names and let the required_reading section guide the stitch agent to find them. I know the use case and test suite files exist based on the spec status, so I'll reference those directly rather than guessing at exact PRD numbering.\n\nSince all four checksum utilities follow nearly identical patterns, I'll structure the task to implement them together as a cohesive set rather than treating them separately. the core logic is identical across all four—read input, compute the hash using the appropriate algorithm, output in `hash  filename` format, and support `--check` mode for verification. I'll structure the requirements around this shared behavior rather than individual utility specs, which gives me exactly 4 requirements: hashing with the specific algorithm, output formatting, checksum verification mode, and SIGPIPE handling. Now I need to guess at the PRD paths and identify the test suite files so the stitch agent can locate everything. I'm mapping out the likely PRD numbering based on the release history—each utility family probably has its own PRD, so the checksum utilities would fall somewhere in the prd020+ range. Rather than guessing exact paths, I'll reference the PRD directory generically and let the stitch agent locate the right files. Since all four checksum utilities share nearly identical implementation patterns, I'll propose building them together in a single task.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27607,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27607},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3d50dbf9-ecc8-4948-9326-1d646d4520dd","uuid":"6682486c-2a0